PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core setup undisputed: Lyz:HaloTag-eGFP (neutrophils) + mpeg1:SNAPtag-DsRed (macrophages); supplied ligand is chloroalkane-Cy5 (Halo-specific, exc ~630 nm). After pulse/wash at 48 hpf and imaging at 4 dpf post-injury, neutrophils carry both eGFP (488 nm) and covalently bound Cy5 (630 nm); macrophages carry only DsRed (559 nm).  
Disagreement centers on “first get signals from”: majority (colleagues 1-4,6,10,11) read it as “which wavelengths ultimately work” → G (all three). Minority (5,8,9,12,15) read it literally as temporal order after injury → neutrophils arrive first, so only 488 nm + 630 nm (D). A few (7,13,14) claim Cy5 signal fades post-wash → E.  
Strongest evidence favors D: tail-fin injury model is classic for sequential leukocyte recruitment; “first” is superfluous unless it encodes timing. No data support Cy5 degradation. Unresolved: exact filter sets or possible FRET, but irrelevant to ordering logic. My prior stance unchanged: D.}

